instances of "easy" but at least humanly possible
ones).

Any real "C" guru willing to tackle _that_
"structure of the entire source code kit" can of
worms?

2) NetHack "needs" IMO either

a) a total "rewrite for maintainability" in its
existing programming language, C, or probably better
yet

b) a total slavishly behavior maintaining conversion
to a programming language where software maintenance
is simply much, much easier.

For example, in an object oriented language,
everything about a monster, its text symbol and
color, its tile data, its relation to weaker and
tamer monsters of the same monster family, its
peculiar behavior, its set of flags, and so on,
would be in a single object for that monster, and
more individualistic monsters could derive most of
their characteristics from less individualistic
monsters (a gnome is a prototypical gnome, plus
weapons capabilities, while a gnome wizard is a
prototype gnome, plus wizard capabilities, e.g.)

C++, Java, Euclid, and OCAML, at least, and OO PERL,
maybe, are all signaling for attention in this
regard.

Java holds pride of place in that list mainly
because by intent of its design it would lose most
of the platform portability issues that plague
NetHack.

It is also a big win in particular because Java
seems to be firmly in place as the mobile NetHack
systems' internal software system of choice already,
unless I've missed some big players somewhere (quite
possible), making it easy to add NetHack to new
mobile "small, portable" computing devices.

Either would make NetHack community member proposed
contributions to the game source code easier and
thus more common, and thus return us to the era that
gave the game its name, ever inflowing hacks to the
source code from contributers all over the Net.



